---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
const pageSlug = 'compare-calculator-vault';
const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Vireault vs calculator vault apps',
  description: 'Why disguise-only calculator vaults differ from an encrypted local Android vault like Vireault.',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Vireault' },
  datePublished: '2026-11-05',
  dateModified: '2026-11-05',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://vireault.app/compare/calculator-vault',
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Vireault vs calculator vault apps"
  description="Calculator vault apps hide behind a keypad UI. Vireault encrypts files with AES-256-GCM on device. Here is a fair comparison."
  path="/compare/calculator-vault"
  pageSlug={pageSlug}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={articleLd}
  article={{ published: '2026-11-05', modified: '2026-11-05' }}
>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/compare', label: 'Compare' }, { label: 'Calculator vaults' }]} />
    <article class="section prose" style="max-width:none">
      <h1>Vireault vs calculator vault apps</h1>
      <p class="lead">“Calculator vault” apps disguise a private gallery behind a calculator icon or keypad. Disguise can deter casual snooping, but it is not the same thing as documented on-device encryption.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Topic</th>
              <th>Vireault</th>
              <th>Typical calculator vault</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Main protection</td>
              <td>Encryption + unlock secret</td>
              <td>UI disguise + passcode gate (quality varies widely)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>App appearance</td>
              <td>Standard vault app icon/name</td>
              <td>Often looks like a calculator</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Cryptography</td>
              <td>Argon2id, AES-256-GCM, encrypted index</td>
              <td>Some apps encrypt; many only hide. Read each listing carefully.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Account</td>
              <td>No account</td>
              <td>Mixed; some are local, some push sign-in or ads</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Backup</td>
              <td>Encrypted backup file + restore</td>
              <td>Inconsistent across apps</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <Cta pageSlug={pageSlug} heading="Choose encryption over disguise alone" />
      <h2>When a calculator vault may be enough</h2>
      <p>If your threat model is a roommate glancing at your phone, a disguised app might be adequate. If you worry about someone browsing device storage, backups or leftover gallery copies, prefer an app that encrypts file bytes and names.</p>
      <h2>What Vireault still does not do</h2>
      <p>Pro can disguise the launcher icon (including a calculator alias) and offers hidden-list / decoy vaults. Those are convenience and casual-denial features — they are not forensic-proof, and the core bet remains cryptography and local control.</p>
      <p>Next reads: <a href="/features/encryption">encryption feature</a>, <a href="/guides/safest-private-gallery-android">safest private gallery checklist</a>, <a href="/compare/keepsafe">vs Keepsafe</a>.</p>
    </article>
    <Cta pageSlug={pageSlug} />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
